userStories.description:
**Goal** — Retain typed MCP connection specifications and scopes when parsing project and plugin catalog entries.

**Motivation** — The catalog parses MCP entries and drops the useful half.

**Interface** — `McpServerResource` (`packages/nax/src/resources/types.ts`) target:

```ts
export type McpServerSpec =
  | {
      readonly transport: "stdio";
      readonly command: string;
      readonly args: readonly string[];
      readonly env: Readonly<Record<string, string>>;
    }
  | {
      readonly transport: "http" | "sse";
      readonly url: string;
      readonly headers: Readonly<Record<string, string>>;
    };

export type McpServerScope = "local" | "project" | "user" | "plugin";

export interface McpServerResource {
  readonly kind: "mcp";
  readonly name: string;
  readonly transport: "stdio" | "http" | "sse";
  readonly scope: McpServerScope;
  readonly server: McpServerSpec;
  readonly path: string;
  readonly origin: ResourceOrigin;
}
```

**Approach** — Parsing portion of **3. Catalog MCP entries keep their connection spec (US-003 parsing, US-004 discovery).**

- `parseMcpJson(path, text, origin, scope)` (`src/resources/parse-mcp.ts`) gains the scope
  parameter. Per entry: `type` `"http"` or `"sse"` needs a string `url` (headers default to
  `{}`); otherwise a string `command` makes a stdio entry (`args` default `[]`, `env`
  default `{}`). `args` that is not an array of strings, `env` or `headers` that is not an
  object of strings, or an http/sse entry with no string `url` yields a `warn` diagnostic
  `[resources] Unsupported MCP entry <name> in <path>` and no resource (today's message).
- A new `parseMcpServers(path, servers, origin, scope)` holds the per-entry loop so that
  `~/.claude.json` reuses it on an already-parsed object; `parseMcpJson` parses the text and
  calls it.

Pass scope project or plugin from claude-sources.ts. Export parseMcpServers, McpServerSpec and McpServerScope from src/resources/index.ts in this story.

**Scope** — In: resource types, shared per-entry parser, existing project/plugin scope assignment and typed fixture migrations. Every parseMcpJson call in test/unit/resources/parse-mcp.test.ts gains project scope and whole-resource expectations gain scope and server; stdio npx args ["x"] has env {}, HTTP https://h has headers {}, and the "sse wins over command" entry gains url https://s while preserving each transport outcome. The typed plugin MCP literal in test/unit/resources/listing.test.ts gains scope plugin and a stdio x/[]/{} server; its expected text is unchanged here. Out: ~/.claude.json discovery and /plugins wording (US-004), expansion (US-005), merge (US-006), network connections (US-002/007). MCP servers declared inside a Claude Code plugin's `plugin.json` (`mcpServers` field) are not read; only the plugin's `.mcp.json` is. SSE parsing retains metadata for later rejection; SSE MCP transport is not supported anywhere; an `sse` catalog entry is listed as a diagnostic and dropped.

**Goal** — Compose configured/catalog MCP servers into chat as discovered providers on one reusable, lazily connected pool.

**Motivation** — Chat has no MCP at all.

**Approach** — Provider/lifecycle portion of **5. Chat wiring (US-007) and `/mcp` (US-008).** New `packages/nax/src/chat/chat-mcp.ts`:

**Interface**
```ts
export interface ChatMcpServerRow {
  readonly id: string;
  readonly source: McpServerSource;
  readonly plugin?: string;
  readonly transport: "stdio" | "http";
}

export interface ChatMcpState {
  readonly servers: readonly ChatMcpServerRow[];
  readonly diagnostics: readonly ResourceDiagnostic[];
  /** Pool connection events, read live for /mcp. */
  events(): readonly McpServerEvent[];
  /** Tools the lock withheld so far, read live for /mcp. */
  withheld(): readonly McpWithheldEntry[];
}

export interface ChatMcp {
  readonly state: ChatMcpState;
  readonly providers: readonly ToolProvider[];
  close(): Promise<void>;
}

/**
 * One attempt per server. Session open resolves providers one after another, so with the
 * run default (3 attempts, 2 s and 4 s backoff, 15 s connect deadline) each dead server
 * would hold the chat's start for up to ~51 s; with one attempt, up to 15 s each.
 */
export const CHAT_MCP_RETRY = { maxAttempts: 1, baseDelayMs: 0 } as const;

export function createChatMcp(input: {
  readonly config: NaxConfig;
  readonly catalog: ResourceCatalog;
  readonly repoRoot: string;
  readonly env: Readonly<Record<string, string | undefined>>;
}): ChatMcp;

export function formatMcpListing(state: ChatMcpState | undefined): string;
```

- `chat-mcp.ts` exports `_chatMcpDeps = { createMcpPool, createMcpProviders }`, the seam
  `createChatMcp` builds through.
- `createChatMcp` merges (Approach 4), keeps every enabled server, and sets each server's
  `stages` to `["*"]`: `stages` decides which `nax run` stages a server attaches to, and a
  chat session is not a pipeline stage. It builds the pool with
  `createMcpPool({ servers, retry: CHAT_MCP_RETRY })` and the providers with
  `createMcpProviders({ config: { servers }, pool, projectRoot: repoRoot, onWithheld })`,
  where `onWithheld` records the entry keyed by `serverId` plus `name`, replacing an earlier
  entry with the same key: `createMcpProviders` reports withheld tools on every `tools()`
  call and the chat resolves providers again on every session reopen (mode switch, model
  switch, `/clear`, resume), so `withheld()` returns each withheld tool once. `close()`
  closes the pool. With no enabled server it builds no pool and returns no providers.
- A server whose id is `nax-skills` (the skills provider's id) is not attached; `createChatMcp`
  drops it with an `error` diagnostic `[resources] mcp server id nax-skills is reserved`.
- `state.servers` has one row per attached server: `id`, `source` and `plugin` from the
  merge's `sources`, and `transport` `http` when the server has a `url`, otherwise `stdio`.
  `state.events()` returns the pool's `events()` (an empty list when no pool was built).
- `state.diagnostics` is the merge diagnostics followed by the catalog diagnostics whose
  `path` ends in `.mcp.json` or `.claude.json`; `/skills` and `/plugins` do not show
  them for the project and user packages, so `/mcp` is where they are seen (a Claude Code
  plugin's `.mcp.json` diagnostic shows in both `/plugins` and `/mcp`).
- `_chatCommandDeps` gains `env` (default `process.env`); `chatResources` passes it to both
  `loadResourceCatalog` and `createChatMcp`, so tests isolate the Claude config directory
  without touching the process environment.
- `chatResources` (`src/cli/chat.ts`) calls `deps.createChatMcp` after loading the catalog,
  appends its providers after the skills provider, sets `ChatResources.mcp` to its state,
  and makes `teardown` close the MCP pool and then tear down the plugin registry; a pool
  close that rejects still lets the registry tear down. `createChatMcp` throwing tears the
  registry down and rethrows, as a catalog failure does today.
- `ChatResources` (`src/chat/resources.ts`) gains `readonly mcp?: ChatMcpState`; `ChatHost`
  gains `readonly mcp?: ChatMcpState`, set from `deps.resources?.mcp`.
- Connection is lazy, as in `nax run`: the pool connects a server the first time the
  session resolves its tools, at session open. A mode switch reopens the session on the same
  pool, whose memoised entries (including a failed one) are reused.

Export createChatMcp, CHAT_MCP_RETRY, _chatMcpDeps and ChatMcp/ChatMcpState/ChatMcpServerRow from src/chat/index.ts here; US-008 supplies/exports formatMcpListing.

**Scope** — In: chat MCP factory/state contract, skills-first provider composition, env injection, host state exposure, single-attempt pool ownership, reserved-ID diagnostic and ordered robust teardown. US-008 completes/verifies the live state details and formatting; keep the state usable here so this story composes independently. Update _chatCommandDeps fakes in test/unit/cli/chat-resources.test.ts and chat.test.ts with empty-state/no-provider/resolving-close createChatMcp and fixture env; preserve every existing compose assertion. Include both seams: real chatCommand + loadResourceCatalog + createChatMcp from fixture .claude.json to captured pool servers; real createChatHost without open override to captured _openChatSessionDeps.nativeBackend toolProviders. Out: agent ask-rule implementation (US-001), merge algorithms (US-006), slash command/listing behavior (US-008), run/catalog lock refresh (US-009). F5b `chat-subagents` delivers subagents, the `Agent` tool, `/agents` and `agent.modelAliases`; this feature leaves `/plugins` printing `agents N (not delivered yet)`. The interactive system prompt does not list MCP tools beyond what the tool list and each server's own instructions (the existing provider-instructions path) already carry. MCP servers are not hot-reloaded; a changed `.mcp.json` or `~/.claude.json` takes effect at the next `nax chat` start. A per-server enable or disable toggle inside chat is not added; `mcp.servers.<id>.enabled` and removing the catalog entry are the switches.

**Security amendment (2026-10-25, user-approved after a security review)** — createChatMcp is async: it reads the lock through _chatMcpDeps.readLock (default resolveMcpLock(repoRoot)). A catalog server (any source other than config) whose id has no lock entry is neither passed to the pool nor attached, so a repo- or plugin-supplied server is never spawned or contacted, and its expanded ${VAR} values never leave the machine, until nax mcp lock has run. Such a server still gets a state.servers row with locked: false. Configured mcp.servers entries are not gated.
